---
import ContentPageHero from './ContentPageHero.astro';

const ceilingCards = [
    {
        label: 'Capacity',
        reality:
            'ChatGPT stores roughly 1,200 words of memory. Claude caps auto-loaded memory at 200 lines. Useful, yes. But these are post-it notes, not a professional knowledge base.',
    },
    {
        label: 'Depth',
        reality:
            'They know your name and your language preference. They do not know why your team made the architectural decision it made last quarter, or how your deploy process actually works.',
    },
    {
        label: 'Staleness',
        reality:
            'Memory that worked last month becomes confidently wrong when your circumstances change. Even leading memory frameworks acknowledge this as unsolved at scale.',
    },
    {
        label: 'Portability',
        reality:
            'Your memories belong to the platform. When users demanded exports in early 2026, the data they got was practically unusable anywhere else. The more you invest, the harder it is to leave.',
    },
];
---

<div class="grid gap-10 md:gap-12">
    <ContentPageHero eyebrow="The problem">
        <Fragment slot="title">AI that isn't yours.</Fragment>
    </ContentPageHero>

    <!-- Opening: the personal frustration -->
    <section class="max-w-[46rem]">
        <div class="grid gap-5 text-lg leading-[1.8] text-[#4f4438]">
            <p class="m-0">
                You use AI every day. It helps you write, think, debug, and
                move faster. It is genuinely useful. But at the end of a long
                day of working alongside the most powerful technology you have
                ever touched -- it does not know you any better than it did
                yesterday.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                It does not know your codebase, your business, your team, or
                the way you think. It cannot, because it was built to serve
                everyone. And an AI that serves everyone cannot go deep for
                anyone.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Beat 1: The personalization ceiling -->
    <section
        class="grid gap-5 border-t border-t-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] pt-6"
    >
        <div class="grid max-w-[44rem] gap-2.5">
            <p class="eyebrow">The ceiling</p>
            <h2
                class="m-0 font-[Cormorant_Garamond,serif] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3vw,2.55rem)] leading-[0.98] text-[#2b241d]"
            >
                Memory is not understanding.
            </h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[#5b4f43] leading-relaxed max-w-[46rem]">
                The platforms are getting better at remembering you. ChatGPT
                has memory and projects. Claude synthesizes context across
                conversations. Third-party tools like Mem0 add persistence to
                almost any model. This is real progress. But there is a ceiling,
                and it is lower than it looks.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
            {
                ceilingCards.map((item) => (
                    <article class="grid gap-2 rounded-[1.2rem] border border-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] bg-[rgba(255,252,247,0.44)] p-5">
                        <h3 class="m-0 font-[IBM_Plex_Mono,monospace] text-[0.68rem] tracking-[0.18em] text-[#7a6d60] uppercase">
                            {item.label}
                        </h3>
                        <p class="m-0 text-[#4f4438] leading-relaxed">
                            {item.reality}
                        </p>
                    </article>
                ))
            }
        </div>
        <aside
            class="grid max-w-[46rem] gap-2 border-l-2 border-l-[rgba(21,74,122,0.18)] pl-5 mt-2"
        >
            <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[1.8] text-[#564a3f]">
                The difference between "recalls a fact about you" and "deeply
                understands your work" is not a feature gap. It is an
                architecture gap. These platforms were built to serve millions
                of people well. They were not built to go
                deep for one person. And that is exactly what a professional
                needs.
            </p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- Beat 2: The depth you're missing -->
    <section
        class="grid gap-5 border-t border-t-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] pt-6 max-w-[46rem]"
    >
        <div class="grid gap-2.5">
            <p class="eyebrow">The gap</p>
            <h2
                class="m-0 font-[Cormorant_Garamond,serif] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3vw,2.55rem)] leading-[0.98] text-[#2b241d]"
            >
                Think about what your AI has never seen.
            </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 text-lg leading-[1.8] text-[#4f4438]">
            <p class="m-0">
                Five years of notes. Your email archive. Your draft documents,
                your internal wikis, your Slack threads. The decisions your team
                made last quarter and the reasoning behind them. The patterns in
                how you actually work -- not what you told a chat window once,
                but the full texture of your professional life.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                A professional-grade AI should know these things. It should
                understand the context behind your questions, not just the
                questions themselves. It should build on last month's work
                instead of starting fresh. It should get meaningfully better the
                more you use it -- not just at recalling facts, but at
                understanding what matters to you and why.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Power users of today's AI consistently describe the experience
                the same way: "meeting a stranger who has read a few notes
                about you." The recall is there. The understanding is not. And
                for serious professional work, the understanding is what counts.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Beat 3: Ownership and trust -->
    <section
        class="grid gap-5 border-t border-t-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] pt-6 max-w-[46rem]"
    >
        <div class="grid gap-2.5">
            <p class="eyebrow">The tradeoff you shouldn't have to make</p>
            <h2
                class="m-0 font-[Cormorant_Garamond,serif] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3vw,2.55rem)] leading-[0.98] text-[#2b241d]"
            >
                The deeper you go, the more ownership matters.
            </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 text-lg leading-[1.8] text-[#4f4438]">
            <p class="m-0">
                The more personal your AI becomes -- the more it knows about
                your work, your decisions, your domain -- the more it matters
                who controls that knowledge. Today, the answer is: not you.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Your memories live on someone else's servers. Your context is
                governed by someone else's retention policies. If you pay for a
                consumer tier, your conversations may train their next model by
                default. Enterprise agreements offer real protections, but they
                are policies you negotiate, not properties of the system you
                own. And every month you invest in one platform makes the next
                one harder to reach.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                For a personal AI that actually works at a professional grade --
                one that holds your institutional knowledge, your decision
                history, your domain expertise -- "trust us" is not a good
                enough answer. You need architecture that makes the question
                irrelevant. Your data, on your infrastructure, under your
                control. Not because the providers are bad actors. Because for
                work that matters, ownership is not optional.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The landscape -->
    <section
        class="grid gap-5 border-t border-t-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] pt-6 max-w-[46rem]"
    >
        <div class="grid gap-2.5">
            <p class="eyebrow">The landscape</p>
            <h2
                class="m-0 font-[Cormorant_Garamond,serif] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3vw,2.55rem)] leading-[0.98] text-[#2b241d]"
            >
                There are tools that promise this. Most of them are not it.
            </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 text-lg leading-[1.8] text-[#4f4438]">
            <p class="m-0">
                The open-source AI space is moving fast. Agent runtimes like
                OpenClaw and self-improving harnesses like Hermes are genuinely
                impressive -- open, model-agnostic, and capable. Frameworks like
                Letta bring real stateful agent infrastructure. These tools are
                worth knowing about.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                But a runtime is not a system. A harness is not an environment.
                And an agent that can execute tasks is not the same thing as a
                professional-grade AI that holds durable state, runs background
                work, gives you structured review surfaces, and connects
                securely to other nodes when you are ready for that.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Most of these tools give you a powerful engine and leave you to
                build the car. You wire up the database, the notifications, the
                search, the UI, the coordination layer. Some users enjoy that
                assembly. But for a professional who needs a system that works
                on day one and compounds from there, assembly is not the point.
                The point is the work.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The turn -->
    <section
        class="grid gap-5 border-t border-t-[rgba(120,113,108,0.14)] pt-6 max-w-[46rem]"
    >
        <div class="grid gap-2.5">
            <p class="eyebrow">The alternative</p>
            <h2
                class="m-0 font-[Cormorant_Garamond,serif] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3vw,2.55rem)] leading-[0.98] text-[#2b241d]"
            >
                What if your AI was actually yours?
            </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 text-lg leading-[1.8] text-[#4f4438]">
            <p class="m-0">
                Not a runtime you configure. A system you own. An opinionated
                seed -- durable state, async execution, review surfaces,
                notifications, search -- that works on day one and is designed
                to be forked, reshaped, and made entirely yours.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Your knowledge base, your history, your patterns -- with no
                ceiling on how deep the understanding can go. Not 1,200 words
                of memory. The whole picture.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Model agnostic. Use the best frontier model when the task
                demands it. Run local when nothing should leave the building.
                Switch providers without losing anything. The privacy is not a
                policy you negotiate -- it is how the system works.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Transparent at every layer. No black boxes, no telemetry you
                did not opt into, no hosted backend you cannot inspect. Every
                piece of state, every decision, every interaction -- yours to
                read, yours to understand, yours to change.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                Professional-grade. Built for serious work by someone doing
                serious work. And when you are ready, your node connects to
                other nodes -- your team, your org, your collaborators --
                through a secure channel that keeps what is personal personal
                and what is shared shared.
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
                That is what GalexC is.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
